1. Inspect CSV structure: Read offline_data/regional_sales.csv: list all column names, total row count, date range (min/max of the month column), unique regions, unique product lines, and print first 5 rows as sample → db_agent
2. Compute 24-month mean & anomaly multiples: Using the CSV, for each (region × product_line) compute the 24-month rolling mean of monthly sales, then compute the ratio = month_sales / 24-month_mean for every month. Identify the top-3 (region, product_line, month) combinations with the highest ratio. Also extract the return_rate for those specific months. Save intermediate results to a CSV → code_agent
3. Assess demand vs channel stuffing: For the top-3 anomaly combinations, compare the anomaly-month return_rate against the 24-month average return_rate for the same (region, product_line). Also check whether the month after the anomaly shows a sales drop (negative growth). Write a short analytical note (as a Python string or small CSV) summarizing whether the spike looks like genuine demand or channel stuffing → code_agent
4. Write Markdown report: Write a complete Markdown report to disk (e.g. /workspace/reports/regional_sales_anomaly_report.md). Requirements: (1) H1 title; (2) at least one data table showing the top-3 combos with their ratio, month, return_rate, and 24-month avg return_rate; (3) every key number annotated with source in the format '（来源：offline_data/regional_sales.csv 的 <列名>，<聚合口径与样本行数>）' or '（来源：由前述两行计算）'; (4) a concluding paragraph at the end judging demand vs channel stuffing; (5) all data from the local CSV only. Save the file and return its absolute path → code_agent
